body:
Concrete additive graphics compiler interface proposal (owner ACK for wiring, no compiler edit/build yet). Reuse struct tegu_mali_specialization from mali_compute_compiler.h: {uint32_t id,size; uint8_t data[8];}; do not define a conflicting copy or alter its layout. Graphics header may forward-declare it, with callers including compute header for the definition. Add these symbols; preserve all existing exports and default behavior:

int tegu_mali_compile_generic_pair_specialized(
 const void *vertex_spirv,size_t vertex_size,
 const void *fragment_spirv,size_t fragment_size,
 const struct tegu_mali_specialization *vertex_constants,size_t vertex_count,
 const struct tegu_mali_specialization *fragment_constants,size_t fragment_count,
 struct tegu_mali_graphics *vertex,struct tegu_mali_graphics *fragment,
 struct tegu_mali_graphics_pair2 *pair,struct tegu_mali_generic_plan *plan,
 char *error,size_t error_size);

int tegu_mali_compile_generic_pair_specialized_rt(
 const void *vertex_spirv,size_t vertex_size,
 const void *fragment_spirv,size_t fragment_size,
 const struct tegu_mali_specialization *vertex_constants,size_t vertex_count,
 const struct tegu_mali_specialization *fragment_constants,size_t fragment_count,
 struct tegu_mali_graphics *vertex,struct tegu_mali_graphics *fragment,
 struct tegu_mali_graphics_pair2 *pair,struct tegu_mali_generic_plan *plan,
 unsigned rt,char *error,size_t error_size);

An optional matching tegu_mali_compile_generic_pair_specialized_tile_rt has the SAME signature as specialized_rt and existing experimental tile policy; do not enable tile production merely to obtain specialization. GenericPlan v1 and all code/result layouts stay unchanged. Existing entrypoints call defaults (NULL,0 per stage); specialized entry with both counts0 must reproduce their outputs byte-for-byte. MRT selection retains rt0..7 and existing reserved semantics.

Each stage gets an independent SpecId namespace. Each table has <=64 entries, unique IDs within that stage, scalar size1/2/4/8, little-endian bytes; count0 permits NULL. Same ID with different bytes across VS/FS is valid. Before Mesa, validate table spans/nonoverlap with outputs and declared SPIR-V scalar type/size (Boolean uses Vulkan VkBool32 representation), so malformed entries do not trigger a backend assertion. Reject invalid/duplicate/unknown-for-that-stage IDs precisely; Rust therefore filters a shared plan specialization key against EACH selected stage specialization declaration instead of flattening stages and taking the first matching ID. Remaining unprovided declarations retain SPIR-V defaults.

Implementation must feed each validated nir_spirv_specialization to its spirv_to_nir invocation BEFORE NIR folding, resource reflection, varying linkage and output selection. The returned native generic plan describes that specialized pair, not the unspecialized pipeline. Frontend obtains actual bound sampler facts (linear stays linear), the selected translator variant modules, and exact plan.specialization bytes together. Never fabricate nearest facts or build sampler bit words independently in C. A separate additive Rust plan/specialization sidecar API is appropriate; do not grow existing GenericPlan in place. Refuse unsupported variant/auxiliary requirements until wired, with command error rather than a successful omitted draw.

Cache identity must include exact selected VS/FS module bytes, both stage specialization tables (canonical ID order), target selection/tile mode, compiler ABI version and existing relevant pipeline state. Specialization can change live binding sets, so revalidate native reflection against the SAME bound variant plan and descriptor facts on every selected compiled variant. Tests: same SpecId different values VS/FS, actual nearest/linear sampler metadata and resulting specialized NIR/ISA, specialization-dependent dead resource removal, empty-table equivalence, defaults/explicit defaults equivalence, invalid sizes/duplicates/wrong-stage IDs with empty outputs, and actual nondefault sampler pipeline through final snapshot+native compile+descriptor assembly. Function constants already baked into SPIR-V are separate from these runtime sampler/texture specialization constants.
